  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nOur weather through the rest of the week will be characterized\nby temperatures increasing by about 2 to 4 degrees each day, as\nwell as an incrementally increasing in southwesterly breezes\nthrough Thursday. By Friday, we may even see a few sites make a\nrun towards 70 degrees south of the Kankakee River -- it really\ndoesn't get much better than this at the end of November! \n\nHave continued to undercut NBM-delivered dewpoints and have \nboosted winds a bit, particularly on Wednesday as a robust low- \nlevel jet develops across central/eastern Iowa and southwest \nWisconsin. While a persistent subsidence inversion is forecast \nto mute mixing depths (coupled with a likely increase in high- \nlevel cloud cover), soundings nonetheless suggests mixing \nheights will be deep enough to transport some 25 to perhaps 30 \nmph gusts. The breeziest conditions are expected across \nnorthwest Illinois. Suppose there could be a modestly increased \nfire danger given the recent dry conditions as well. \n\nThursday may be similarly breezy, which will help boost highs\ninto the 60s area-wide. By Friday, 925 mb temperatures will be\npushing +13 C which will support mid and upper 60 degree high\ntemperatures--perhaps locally near 70 in our far southwest. \n\nGuidance is in reasonably good agreement over the weekend,\ntracking a robust shortwave and attendant surface low across the\nPlains and northeast through the Upper Midwest. This will bring\nour next (brief) chance for some rainfall, although the fast-\nmoving nature of the upper trough and limited deep moisture\nwill temper overall amounts. The trailing cold front will bring\na return to more seasonable conditions on Sunday and early next\nweek. \n\nCarlaw",
